> >
> > I want to model magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) data with INLA.
> > Standard MRI data is contaminated with Rician noise. At high signal-
> > to-noise ratios the Rician distribution is virtually indistinguishable
> > from a Gaussian, yet I am interested in the very low SNR regime.
> > Thus, I need to implement a new likelihood / family. As far as I
> > understand, this is not possible via R but should be feasible by
> > cloning the Git repository and working on the source code.
> > While this seems like a slightly daunting task for an outsider, I am
> > very motivated to give it a try.
> > Can you point me to any roadmap, forum posts I have overlooked or
> > additional documentation for implementing a new likelihood?
> > Detailed specifications for the desired likelihood:
> >  * Non-central chi distribution (more general case encompassing the
> > Rician)
> >  * Exponential link function between the linear predictor and the non-
> > centrality parameter (like in the standard “poisson” family)
> >  * Truncation on the left as signal values below a certain threshold
> > are considered background noise and do not enter the analysis.
> > Am I overlooking any limitations regarding the class of distributions
> > that can be incorporated into INLA?
> > Best regards and thanks for your support!
